␀␀properties␀␀title␀␀␀description␀x-espos-version␀x-espos-runtime␀default␀minimum␀maximum␀maxLength␀␀␀enum␀␀␀␀contentEncoding␀blob␀␀␀␀x-espos-type␀␀␀␀x-espos-maxBytes␀␀␀␀x-espos-unit␀␀␀␀writeOnly␀␀␀password␀␀␀␀x-espos-secret␀␀x-espos-restartRequired␀readOnly␀␀␀␀x-espos-group␀␀␀x-espos-displayMultiplier␀␀␀x-espos-displayOffset␀␀␀table␀␀␀x-espos-format␀␀x-espos-columns␀additionalProperties␀␀␀␀HTTP server␀Built-in web server serving the UI and the REST API.␀␀␀␀Network␀What the device is called on the network, whichever interface carries it.␀␀␀Firmware updates␀␀␀␀Over-the-air updates: where to look for new firmware and how careful to be about a freshly installed image.␀Clock␀␀␀Where the device learns what time it is. Everything espOS publishes is UTC; the timezone below is for displays only.␀␀␀␀WiFi␀␀␀␀Station networks in priority order (slot 0 first), addressing, reconnect policy and the provisioning access point.␀␀Station enabled␀Connect to the configured networks. Off = radio idle (portal still available if enabled).␀␀␀Hostname (moved to Network)␀Superseded by net.hostname. A value here is moved there at the next boot and this key is cleared; set the hostname under Network.␀␀␀Network 1 SSID␀␀Network 1 password␀␀Empty for an open network, else 8..63 characters (WPA).␀Network 1 BSSID pin␀Optional aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff to pin one access point (no roaming).␀␀␀␀Network 2 SSID␀␀Network 2 password␀␀Network 2 BSSID pin␀Network 3 SSID␀␀Network 3 password␀␀Network 3 BSSID pin␀Network 4 SSID␀␀Network 4 password␀␀Network 4 BSSID pin␀Addressing␀␀dhcp = take an address from the router. static = use the address below; applied before every connection, so a change takes effect at the next (re)connect or reboot.␀␀␀␀dhcp␀␀␀␀Static IP address␀␀␀IPv4 address for ip_mode = static. Invalid or empty falls back to DHCP with a warning in the log.␀␀␀Static netmask␀␀e.g. 255.255.255.0. Required with a static address.␀Static gateway␀␀Default router. Empty = none (the device then reaches its own subnet only).␀Static DNS 1␀␀␀␀Primary resolver with a static address. Empty = the gateway.␀␀␀␀Static DNS 2␀␀␀␀Backup resolver with a static address. Empty = none.␀␀␀␀Reconnect backoff cap␀␀␀Exponential backoff between rounds over all networks, 1 s doubling up to this cap, В±25 % jitter.␀␀␀DHCP timeout␀␀␀␀Associated but no IP after this long counts as a failed attempt.␀␀␀␀Connect timeout␀Safety net if the driver never reports a result.␀␀␀␀Provisioning access point␀␀␀Bring up the setup access point when no network is configured or nothing connects for a while.␀␀Portal after␀␀␀␀Seconds without a connection before the access point comes up (0 networks configured: immediately).␀Access point while online␀␀␀Raise the setup access point even when Ethernet (or another transport) is carrying the network. Off: a device reachable over Ethernet does not advertise one.␀␀␀Access point SSID␀␀␀Empty = espOS-<last 4 hex of the device MAC>.␀␀␀Access point password␀␀␀Empty = open network. 8..63 characters for WPA2.␀␀␀␀Power save␀␀WiFi modem power save. 'none' keeps latency lowest.␀Set the clock from NTP␀␀Ask an NTP server for the time once the network is up. Off = the clock comes only from the SignalK stream or a manual set, which is what a device on a boat network with no internet route wants.␀␀␀NTP server␀␀Host name or IP. Empty and with DHCP off, nothing is asked.␀pool.ntp.org␀␀␀␀Second NTP server␀␀␀Optional fallback, used when the first does not answer.␀Use the NTP server DHCP offers␀␀Many routers hand out an NTP server with the lease, and it is usually the router itself вЂ” reachable even when the boat has no internet. A DHCP-supplied server replaces the list above and is refreshed on every new lease.␀␀␀sk_fallback␀Fall back to the SignalK clock␀␀While the clock is unset, take the time from navigation.datetime on the SignalK stream. The server usually has GPS time, so a device with no internet route still gets a usable clock. Dropped as soon as NTP succeeds.␀Timezone (POSIX TZ)␀For displays only вЂ” nothing espOS sends is ever in local time. POSIX form, e.g. UTC0, CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3 for central Europe. Note the POSIX sign convention: the offset is west-positive, so CET (UTC+1) is written -1.␀␀␀Where to look for updates␀␀␀url = the manifest URL below. signalk = ask the SignalK server this device is already connected to, so a device that finds its server also finds its updates and nothing has to be typed per device (needs a fleet-update plugin on that server).␀␀␀Manifest URL␀␀␀␀http(s) URL of a version manifest (docs/ota.md). Empty = manual updates from a URL only. Ignored when the source above is signalk.␀␀Manifest path on the server␀Path appended to the SignalK server's address when the source is signalk. The default is where the fleet-update plugin serves it.␀␀␀/plugins/signalk-espos-updates/manifest.json␀␀␀␀Channel␀Which channel of the manifest to follow, e.g. stable or beta.␀␀␀Check automatically␀Fetch the manifest after boot and every check interval.␀Check interval␀␀Install automatically␀␀␀Install a newer build from the manifest as soon as it is found (device reboots). Off = only show it.␀␀␀␀Skip TLS certificate check␀␀Accept any https certificate (self-signed boat servers). Images are still signature-checked.␀␀␀␀Rollback timeout␀␀␀␀A freshly installed image must reach the network within this time, else the device rolls back to the previous firmware.␀Hostname␀␀␀␀DHCP and mDNS name (<hostname>.local); also the SignalK source label and the name in the server's access-request list. Empty = espos-<last 4 hex of the device MAC>.␀␀␀␀TCP port␀␀␀␀Port the HTTP server listens on.␀␀␀␀API key␀Shared secret for the web UI and the REST API, sent as Authorization: Bearer or exchanged for a login cookie. Empty = no authentication, the API is open to anyone on the network. 8вЂ“64 characters; the Config page can generate one. Changing it logs every browser out.␀Login session lifetime␀␀How long a browser login (the espos_sid cookie) stays valid. Sessions live in RAM; a reboot ends them all.␀␀W (%lu) %s: nvs_flash_deinit_partition('%s'): %s
